---
title: Newsletters and campaigns
name: Newsletters
description: Save and review campaigns, move an audience with its consent, and rehearse signup and delivery in the local inbox.
category: Guides
---

Postboi keeps newsletter campaigns as saved drafts. Create an audience, check its consent,
write a draft and review its current revision before launching. The Postboi provider's
SDK and CLI use the same API as the dashboard.

## A reviewed campaign

```typescript
import Postboi from 'postboi'

const mail = new Postboi({ token: process.env.POSTBOI_TOKEN })
const list = await mail.lists.create('Weekly news')
await mail.newsletters.configure({
  list_id: list.id,
  slug: 'weekly-news',
  postal_address: 'Your organisation postal address',
  public_signup: true
})

const draft = await mail.campaigns.create({
  name: 'November update',
  list_id: list.id,
  sender: 'news@yourdomain.example',
  subject: 'This month',
  body: '<h1>Hello {name|there}</h1><p>Your news.</p>'
})
const review = await mail.campaigns.review(draft.id)
const html = await mail.campaigns.preview(draft.id)
// Check review.blocking and inspect html before launching.
await mail.campaigns.test(draft.id, 'reviewer@yourdomain.example')
await mail.campaigns.launch(draft.id, {
  revision: draft.revision,
  scheduled_at: '2027-01-12T09:00:00Z',
  timezone: 'Europe/London'
})
```

`test` sends to the explicit email only. `sample_contact_id` can supply a real audience
member's personalization while keeping that review address as the destination.
`update` replaces a draft and needs its current `revision`. Launched content is immutable;
`duplicate` makes another draft. Pausing leaves unsent work available for resume, and
canceling stops unsent work.

`report` separates provider acceptance from confirmed delivery. Opens are observed tracking
signals, not proof that a person read a message. `resend(id, idempotency_key)` creates a
new draft from confirmed delivered, tracked recipients without an observed open. Review and
launch that new draft separately.

`recipients(id, { offset, limit })` pages recipient results. `export` returns a CSV page;
use its offset and limit to download another page.

## Audience and consent

```typescript
await mail.newsletters.signup(list.id, {
  email: 'reader@example.com', name: 'Reader', consent: true
})
const page = await mail.newsletters.audience(list.id, { search: 'reader', offset: 0 })
// page.contacts, total, audience_total, next_offset (25 per page)
const tag = await mail.newsletters.tags.create(list.id, 'Events', {
  contact_ids: [page.contacts[0].id], event_id: 'event-request-42'
})
await mail.newsletters.segments.create(list.id, 'Events readers', {
  include_tag_ids: [tag.tag_id]
})
```

Audience contacts retain the global `name` and `data`. `newsletter_name`, `newsletter_data`
and `provenance` carry this list's imported name, custom fields and original source evidence;
the latter two are JSON strings or null. Follow `next_offset` to inspect the next page.

Signup requests a confirmation email. Reading a confirmation link does not subscribe;
the recipient confirms with a deliberate POST. Existing opt-outs and profile data are
preserved by the request. Signed preference links let readers stop their subscription.
There is no admin shortcut for confirming a recipient through this SDK.

For an existing Mailchimp CSV, preview before applying:

```typescript
const staged = await mail.newsletters.imports.preview(list.id, {
  csv, filename: 'audience.csv', source_profile: 'mailchimp', default_status: 'pending',
  consent_confirmed: true
})
let remaining = staged.import.total
while (remaining > 0) {
  const page = await mail.newsletters.imports.apply(list.id, staged.import.id)
  remaining = page.remaining
}
```

Choose `consent_confirmed: true` only for consent you already hold. Preview stages and
counts changes without changing the audience. Apply is paged and safe to repeat. Importing
never sends mail, and an imported subscribed row cannot revive an existing opt-out.
`source_profile` selects `generic`, `mailchimp`, `activecampaign`, `beehiiv`, `substack`,
`kit`, `mailerlite`, `buttondown`, `ghost`, `emailoctopus` or `listmonk`. It changes field
suggestions and conservative state interpretation, and is retained with original source
evidence. Unknown status or selected opt-out values stay pending. Paid/free membership
never implies permission. Creation and payment dates stay source evidence; explicitly
mapped consent timestamps need an ISO date or timezone. Ambiguous opt-out columns need
an explicit mapping. Valid addresses with metadata that exceeds the supported row, field
or 50-tag limit refuse preview so restrictive facts and exclusion tags are never silently
dropped. The CLI accepts `--profile ghost`; local rehearsal uses the same profiles.

## Reviewed automations

Rules send one message after a trigger tag is added, or after a confirmed signup when
`on_signup` is true. Saving always creates a paused draft. Importing contacts or tags never
enables or triggers a rule.

```typescript
const trigger = await mail.newsletters.tags.create(list.id, 'Event ready')
const rule = await mail.newsletters.rules.create(list.id, {
  name: 'Event follow-up', trigger_tag_id: trigger.tag_id,
  subject: 'Thank you {name|there}', body: '<p>Your follow-up.</p>',
  sender: 'news@yourdomain.example', on_signup: false
})
const preview = await mail.newsletters.rules.preview(list.id, rule.id)
const review = await mail.newsletters.rules.review(list.id, rule.id)
if (review.ready) {
  // Inspect the preview and approve this exact revision deliberately.
  await mail.newsletters.rules.enable(list.id, rule.id, {
    revision: review.revision, confirm: true
  })
}
```

`rules.all/get` inspect saved rules; `rules.update` uses PATCH and requires a complete
rule draft plus its current `revision`. Pause an enabled rule before editing it.
`rules.pause(list_id, rule_id, { revision })` stops unsent work. Enabling again authorizes
future triggers, without automatically replaying prior intents. Preview links are inert.
The service checks current consent, authority, key/grant validity and sender permission
before delivery. `completion_tag_id` optionally records completion without chaining another
rule implicitly. A review does not itself enable anything.

```bash
postboi newsletters rules LIST create --file rule.json --json
postboi newsletters rules LIST review RULE --json
postboi newsletters rules LIST preview RULE --out rule-preview.html
postboi newsletters rules LIST enable RULE --revision 1 --confirm
postboi newsletters rules LIST pause RULE --revision 1
postboi newsletters rules LIST run RULE --local --json
```

The last command exists only for the local inbox. It captures at most 20 pending intents,
rechecking local subscription and suppression before each capture. Add tags or confirm a
new local signup after enabling the rule. The workspace provides the same tag, rule editor,
preview, review, explicit enable and pause controls. Local rules cannot verify hosted API
keys, OAuth grants, sender domains or team authority, and never claim delivery or opens.

## From the terminal

```bash
postboi newsletters configure --file newsletter.json
postboi newsletters imports LIST preview --file audience.csv --status pending --json
postboi newsletters imports LIST apply IMPORT --json
postboi newsletters signup LIST --email reader@example.com --consent
postboi campaigns create --file draft.json --json
postboi campaigns review CAMPAIGN --json
postboi campaigns preview CAMPAIGN --out preview.html
postboi campaigns test CAMPAIGN --email reviewer@example.com
postboi campaigns launch CAMPAIGN --revision 1 --timezone Europe/London --at 2027-01-12T09:00:00Z
postboi campaigns report CAMPAIGN --json
```

The JSON draft uses the same names as the SDK's create input: `name`, `list_id`, `subject`,
`body` and `sender`. The [CLI reference](/cli) lists the controls and audience commands.

## Rehearse in the dev inbox

```bash
postboi dev
postboi lists add 'Weekly news' --local --json
postboi newsletters configure --file newsletter.json --local
postboi newsletters signup LIST --email reader@example.test --consent --local
postboi campaigns create --file draft.json --local --json
postboi campaigns review CAMPAIGN --local
postboi campaigns test CAMPAIGN --email reviewer@example.test --local
postboi campaigns launch CAMPAIGN --revision 1 --timezone UTC --local
postboi campaigns run CAMPAIGN --local
```

Open **Newsletters** from the [dev inbox](/dev-inbox), or go to
`http://localhost:1080/__postboi/newsletters`. Create a list, configure its postal address,
request a signup and open the captured local confirmation. Save and review a draft, capture
a test, then launch its reviewed revision. **Run due local work** processes up to 100
recipients per page. Repeat it until complete; a future schedule stays pending.

For SDK rehearsals, use `new Postboi({ newsletter_local: true })` for list and newsletter
resources. `mail.campaigns` and `mail.newsletters` also use the inbox automatically when
`NODE_ENV=development`. A missing or unreachable inbox refuses the operation without
falling back to the hosted API. Production uses hosted resources; an explicit
`newsletter_local: false` opts out of automatic newsletter capture.

Local previews use your authored HTML and the hosted personalization filters and date
helpers. Hosted rendering also applies your saved brand and letterhead. Review the hosted
campaign before its production launch. Local reports count captures as simulated
acceptance; delivery and opens remain unobserved. Local non-opener resend is unavailable
because a capture cannot establish that someone received or ignored mail.

The rehearsal is in memory and clears when the server restarts. It supports up to 100
lists and campaigns, 10,000 total memberships and CSV files under 2 MB. Use synthetic
addresses and fixtures for this workflow.
